examples.question_text:
The curve has the form $y=k\cos(a(x-b))+c$, where $k>0$, $a>0$ and $0<b<\dfrac{\pi}{2}$. Determine $k$, $a$, $b$ and $c$, then write its equation.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{waveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=10cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black,-{Stealth}},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},xmin=-0.2,xmax=6.65,ymin=-0.4,ymax=6,xtick={0.785398,2.356194,3.926991,5.497787,6.283185},xticklabels={$\frac{\pi}{4}$,$\frac{3\pi}{4}$,$\frac{5\pi}{4}$,$\frac{7\pi}{4}$,$2\pi$},ytick={1,3,5},samples=180,clip=true]
\addplot[waveblue,thick,domain=0:6.283185] {2*cos(deg(2*(x-pi/4)))+3};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
$\begin{aligned}y&=-3\sin\left(2x-\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right)+1\\&=-3\sin\left(2\left(x-\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)\right)+1\end{aligned}$

1. Dilate horizontally by a factor of $\dfrac12$.
2. Translate $\dfrac{\pi}{4}$ right.
3. Dilate vertically by a factor of $3$ and reflect in the $x$-axis.
4. Translate $1$ unit up.

Each dilation precedes its corresponding translation; the reflection precedes the vertical translation.

The amplitude is $3$, the mean position is $y=1$, and the period is $\dfrac{2\pi}{2}=\pi$.

One cycle passes through $(0,4)$, $\left(\dfrac{\pi}{4},1\right)$, $\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2},-2\right)$, $\left(\dfrac{3\pi}{4},1\right)$ and $(\pi,4)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{waveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=10cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black,-{Stealth}},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},xmin=-0.15,xmax=3.45,ymin=-2.8,ymax=4.8,xtick={0.785398,1.570796,2.356194,3.141593},xticklabels={$\frac{\pi}{4}$,$\frac{\pi}{2}$,$\frac{3\pi}{4}$,$\pi$},ytick={-2,1,4},samples=120,clip=true]
\addplot[black,dashed,domain=0:3.141593] {1};
\addplot[waveblue,thick,domain=0:3.141593] {-3*sin(deg(2*x-pi/2))+1};
\addplot[waveblue,only marks,mark=*] coordinates {(0,4) (0.785398,1) (1.570796,-2) (2.356194,1) (3.141593,4)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

On this domain, $\dfrac{\pi}{6}\le2x\le\dfrac{2\pi}{3}$. Sine rises to its maximum and then falls, so check the maximum and both endpoints.

$\begin{aligned}2x&=\dfrac{\pi}{2}\\x&=\dfrac{\pi}{4}\end{aligned}$

This lies in the domain, and $f\left(\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)=3$.

$\begin{aligned}f\left(\dfrac{\pi}{12}\right)&=3\sin\dfrac{\pi}{6}\\&=\dfrac32\end{aligned}$

$\begin{aligned}f\left(\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right)&=3\sin\dfrac{2\pi}{3}\\&=\dfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}\end{aligned}$

The smaller endpoint value is $\dfrac32$.

$\text{Range}=\left[\dfrac32,\,3\right]$

